{
  "summary": "Enforces the caller-provided `max_tx_weight` option in wallet transaction funding when preset inputs already cover the payment amount. Previously, bypassing coin selection caused the final guard to fall back to `MAX_STANDARD_TX_WEIGHT` (400,000 WU), allowing funded transactions to exceed the user's requested weight limit.",
  "problem": "Callers of `fundrawtransaction` (or wallet funding APIs) who specify `max_tx_weight` receive transactions exceeding their limit if the transaction already includes enough preset inputs. When preset inputs suffice, coin selection returns early and the final check checks only against standard transaction weight limits instead of the configured ceiling.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'I reproduced this with 10 legacy inputs and max_tx_weight=4000. Funding succeeded even though the transaction was 6,168 WU after signing. Same result with both add_inputs=false and add_inputs=true.'",
    "Commit message: 'Preset inputs can cover the target without passing coin selection\\'s maximum weight checks. The final guard used MAX_STANDARD_TX_WEIGHT rather than the validated configured maximum.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "The diff was not provided, but metadata indicates a small 17-line change across 2 files including regression tests.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Wallet transaction funding (`fundrawtransaction`) fails to enforce the caller-specified `max_tx_weight` when provided preset inputs already satisfy the target amount. Because coin selection exits early, the wallet's fallback validation checked against standard network weight (400,000 WU) rather than the configured limit, returning transactions larger than requested. This PR changes the post-selection guard to check the user's configured limit against actual signed weight or estimated signed weight, accompanied by regression tests.",
